You are now acting as a fleet allocator. Do NOT generate a behavior tree in this step.

Divide the following mission across the robots that are currently online. You will be asked to
generate each robot's behavior tree separately, afterwards, one robot at a time.

MISSION:
{{ mission }}

ROBOTS ONLINE ({{ robot_count }}):
{% for robot in robots %}
- robot_id: {{ robot.robot_id }}
  name: {{ robot.name }}
  schema: {{ robot.schema }}
{% if robot.actions %}
  supported actions: {{ robot.actions | join(", ") }}
{% else %}
  supported actions: every action in its schema
{% endif %}
  status: {{ robot.status }}{% if robot.battery_pct is not none %}, battery: {{ (robot.battery_pct * 100) | round(0) | int }}%{% endif %}

{% endfor %}
{% if orchard %}
ORCHARD:
- {{ orchard.rows }} rows x {{ orchard.cols }} columns, {{ orchard.tree_count }} trees total
- tree_index runs {{ orchard.tree_index_min }} to {{ orchard.tree_index_max }}
- traversal axis: {{ orchard.traversal_axis }} (robots drive along {{ orchard.line_key }}s)
- aisles are the driving lanes BETWEEN tree {{ orchard.line_key }}s, numbered 1 to {{ orchard.aisle_count }};
  aisle k lies between {{ orchard.line_key }} k and {{ orchard.line_key }} k+1
{% endif %}
{% if suggested_partition %}
SUGGESTED BALANCED SPLIT (already computed for you, use it unless the mission calls for something else):
{% for block in suggested_partition %}
- {{ block.line_key }}s {{ block.line_range[0] }}-{{ block.line_range[1] }}: {{ block.tree_count }} trees (tree_index {{ block.tree_index_range[0] }}-{{ block.tree_index_range[1] }}), interior aisles {{ block.aisles | join(", ") if block.aisles else "none" }}
{% endfor %}
{% endif %}

RULES:
1. Give each robot AT MOST ONE assignment. Do not list a robot twice.
2. Only assign work a robot's schema and supported actions can actually perform. If a robot cannot
   contribute to this mission, leave it out entirely.
3. Two robots must NEVER be assigned the same aisle. Keep each robot's area contiguous.
4. Balance the work by tree count, not by area, unless the mission says otherwise.
5. If the mission names a specific quantity of targets, the assignments together must cover exactly
   that quantity - no more, no less, and no overlap.
6. If only one robot can do the work, give it the entire mission.
7. If no robot can do the work, return an empty assignments list and explain why in unassigned_reason.
8. Each sub_mission must be a complete, self-contained instruction written the way the operator
   would phrase it. The robot generating from it will NOT see this allocation or the other robots'
   assignments, so never write "the rest", "the other half", or "continue from robot 1".

Respond with a single JSON object and nothing else. No prose, no markdown fence, no comments.

{
  "assignments": [
    {
      "robot_id": "<one of the robot_ids listed above>",
      "sub_mission": "<self-contained instruction for this robot>",
      "assigned_aisles": [<aisle numbers this robot owns, may be empty>],
      "assigned_tree_indices": [<tree_index values this robot is responsible for, may be empty>],
      "rationale": "<one short sentence>"
    }
  ],
  "unassigned_reason": null
}
